lls": 0, "edit_calls": 2, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "11.9.0 (/workspace/pi-check/cache2/bin/omp/ab1db22d1d3f60e8f443006a5aa3d5b4158aa3cf/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Shell is mangling indentation; use a file.\nI fixed the bug in `ledger/currency.py`, in `to_cents()`. The old version did `int(amount * 100)`, which cuts off everything after the decimal point. Floats can't store most prices exactly, so `19.99 * 100` comes out as `1998.999\u2026` and became 1998 cents instead of 1999. The same thing broke `0.29` and `4.35`. Because subtotal, discount and tax all start from `to_cents`, that one lost cent caused every failure in the totals tests.\n\nThe new version converts the amount through `Decimal(str(amount))` and rounds half-up to whole cents. For example, `19.99` gives 1999 and `1.005` gives 101. Negative amounts round away from zero.\n\npytest isn't installed here and pip install is blocked, so I couldn't run pytest itself. Instead I imported the test modules and called each test function directly. All four tests in `test_totals.py` and the one in `test_report.py` passed. Run `pytest` in your own environment to confirm.\n\nOne thing to be aware of: the discount and tax steps already rounded half-up, but `to_cents` used to truncate. It now rounds half-up as well, so any input with more than two decimal places will round instead of truncating.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 0, "replies_ok": 10, "replies_throttled": 0, "re